#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158385} :activity/id #uuid "1d574af2-7331-5f7c-b580-2b1b6004da27" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049435 :topic/id #uuid "f51bc61f-8fa1-5d38-b9a3-c8521db4447e" :topic/math-academy-id 2856} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158385 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158506 :tutorial/content "When we have a cube root in an inequality, we can cube both sides of the inequality.\n\nFor example, to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{x-2} \\leq 4,\n$$\n\nwe can cube both sides and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt[3]{x-2})}^{3} & \\le {4}^{3} \\\\ x-2 & \\le 64 \\\\ x & \\le 66.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reason why we're allowed to cube both sides of an inequality is that cubing preserves the order of numbers. That is to say, if $a < b,$ then $a^3 < b^3.$\n\nTo see this concretely, consider the following order of numbers:\n\n$$\n-2 < -1 < 0 < 0.5 < 1 < 2 < 3\n$$\n\nIf we take the cubes of all the numbers above, they stay in the same order:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{-8}_{ (-2)^3 } < \\underbrace{-1}_{ (-1)^3 } < \\underbrace{0}_{ 0^3 } < \\underbrace{0.125}_{ (0.5)^3 } < \\underbrace{1}_{ 1^3 } < \\underbrace{8}_{ 2^3 } < \\underbrace{27}_{ 3^3 }\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "372baf4e-1214-5c31-9cee-48762b863105" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4653 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d46966b9-f04d-56e3-9df8-7ed2c4d8af6b" :step/math-academy-id 468025 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158386}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158386 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157442 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157132 :question/id #uuid "813b7b5f-0da8-5a92-b4ff-f7208c5f059f" :question/math-academy-id "e-7654"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf6a3dbb-52b3-5384-b640-0fbf29a6cc98" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049986}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157891} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157894} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250333} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294915} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294921} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354585}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving a Cube Root"} :step/id #uuid "fd5a0c08-cb93-5a8d-9608-af5e2a089f47" :step/math-academy-id 468026 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158387}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158387 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158504 :tutorial/content "When we have a square root in an inequality, we *can't* always square both sides. To see why not, consider the following inequality:\n\n$$\n-4 \\lt 3\n$$\n\nIf we square both sides, then we get a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4)^{2} & ≮{3}^{2} \\\\ 16 & ≮9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, if we're comparing a square root to a negative number, there's no need to take the square root. The key is to remember that the square root of a number is always positive.\n\n- If a square root is less than (or equal to) a negative number, then there is no solution.\n\nFor example, if\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x+1} < -3,\n$$\n\nthen there is no solution because the square root $\\sqrt{x+1}$ can never be negative.\n\n- If a square root is greater than (or equal to) a negative number, then any number we can substitute into the square root will satisfy the inequality. So, the solution is the domain of the square root.\n\nFor example, if\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x+1} > -3,\n$$\n\nthen the solution consists of all $x$ in the domain of $\\sqrt{x+1}$ because $\\sqrt{x+1}$ is zero or positive for all $x$ in its domain. To find the domain, we recall that the inside of the square root must be greater than or equal to $0{:}$\n\n$$\nx+1 \\geq 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad x \\geq -1\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "21c64dd2-31f7-5c73-a9b1-a82a67d65615" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4651 :tutorial/title "Comparing a Square Root to a Negative Number"} :step/id #uuid "a7b132c4-8966-5562-a68f-5bcc071c84e8" :step/math-academy-id 468029 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158388}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158388 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157443 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157133 :question/id #uuid "bc44bc68-a615-5f25-b686-8a775fcac64c" :question/math-academy-id "e-7655"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24f2c5a5-dc56-5f78-8138-8f7b3a6280e2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157890} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157892} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250334} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294865} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354587} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354588} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354589}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving a Square Root Compared to a Negative Number"} :step/id #uuid "1c597805-9cb4-559b-bb63-8db421b0801d" :step/math-academy-id 468030 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158389}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158389 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158505 :tutorial/content "If we have an inequality that compares a square root to a non-negative number, we can square both sides of the equation.\n\nHowever, there is a catch: when we solve the resulting inequality, we must remember to remove extraneous solutions. We can do this by keeping only the solutions that are in the domain of the square root.\n\nFor example, consider the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x+1} < 3\n$$\n\nIn this inequality, the square root is being compared to a non-negative number $(3).$ So, we can square both sides of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt{x+1})}^{2} & <{3}^{2} \\\\ x+1 & <9 \\\\ x & <8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the square root. To find the domain of the square root, we recall that the argument of the square root must be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx+1 \\geq 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad x \\geq -1\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x < 8,$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty, 8).$ But the domain of the square root is $x \\geq -1,$ which can be expressed as $[-1, \\infty).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the square root, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty, 8) \\cap [-1, \\infty) = [-1, 8)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\in [-1,8),$ which can also be written as $-1 \\leq x < 8.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "17128b58-d757-54db-8cf9-d8f7b07a2f4b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4652 :tutorial/title "Comparing a Square Root to a Positive Number"} :step/id #uuid "3f826d84-2a18-5b72-abc0-6924c5df05de" :step/math-academy-id 468034 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158390}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158390 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157444 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157134 :question/id #uuid "bca45b70-4e79-5baf-a4be-186366cebdd5" :question/math-academy-id "e-7656"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9003081-7cf6-5a99-accf-6a69b65db47c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049597}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157893} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157895} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234468} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250336} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294866} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354591} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354592}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving a Square Root Compared to a Positive Number"} :step/id #uuid "db7196bf-9800-52ad-a813-ef174eb1383c" :step/math-academy-id 468035}] :activity/title "Solving Radical Inequalities" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186157501}]}
